\section{The first idea}
\label{sec:first-idea}

Article-only prose runs between the frames and carries the lecture notes.
It says what the deck is about to do and why, in the student's language.
A section asks before it tells: exercise, then output, then explanation,
then the definition last.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:first-question}
    The try-first question, asked before anything it presupposes has been
    explained.  It stays open --- "what does the program print?", not
    "what is the mistake?" --- so the critical aspect can surface in the
    answers without being pointed at.
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{ModuleLOFirst}.

  Why this example and not another; which misconception it targets; what
  varies and what stays invariant (contrast, generalisation or fusion ---
  the label must match what the example actually varies).  \parencite is
  used only inside an \verb|\ltnote|; the prose cites with \verb|\autocite|.
  Anchor the note where the decision is made, not at the end of the
  passage.
}

Now the program that answers it.  A frame holding a chunk must be
\texttt{[fragile]}, and a theorem-style environment never opens with a
chunk: a short lead-in sentence comes first.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{example}[A program that greets]\label{ex:greeting}
    The program prints one line:
<<[[example.py]]>>=
print("Hej!")
@
    and running it looks like this:
    \runpython[showcommand, chdir]{examples/example.py}
  \end{example}
\end{frame}

The explanation follows the output, not the other way round.  Say what
happened, in the words the student just saw on the screen.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{definition}[The term]
    The definition comes last, once the student has met the thing it
    names.
  \end{definition}
\end{frame}

